<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#congreve-rocket">Glossary</a> · Term · weapon</p><h1 class="enc-title">Congreve rocket</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Congreve rockets</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Congreve rocket was a British military rocket developed by William Congreve in the early nineteenth century, based on rockets used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>. It was inaccurate but had a strong psychological effect. A British-led coalition used Congreve rockets to defeat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire</a>'s army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-katamansu">Battle of Katamansu</a> in 1826.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/congreve-rocket.jpg" alt="Congreve rocket" width="320" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Richie Bendall · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_Congreve_in_1807.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> employed up to 5,000 artillerymen who specialised in rockets with cases of hammered iron, which could travel almost 1,000 yards, while rockets in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> had no iron case and a shorter reach. Several of these rockets were sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> after 1799. William Congreve began work in 1804 by buying the best rockets on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> market, which reached only 600 yards. After experiments at his own expense, and then at the Woolwich Arsenal's laboratory with the permission of Lord Chatham, he was producing 32-pounder rockets with a range of 3,000 yards by the spring of 1806.</p><p>The cases, first of cardboard, were made of sheet iron by 1806. The propellant used the ingredients of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a>, and a wooden stick fixed to the side steadied the flight. Warheads were incendiary, explosive or filled with shot, and the rockets could be fired from a frame, a tripod or even a sloping bank.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> was the main user in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>, and the Royal Marine Artillery became expert in the weapon; the army formed two Rocket Troops of the Royal Horse Artillery on 1 January 1814. The first attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne</a> in 1804 failed in strong winds, and the second Boulogne expedition is usually counted the first success. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-1812">War of 1812</a> rockets were used against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lewes-delaware">Lewes, Delaware</a>, in April 1813 and helped rout the American forces at the Battle of Bladensburg, which led to the burning of Washington, D.C. They served at the bombardment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a> in 1816, against fighting elephants in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-burmese-war">First Anglo-Burmese War</a> in 1825, and in the Opium Wars, the Crimean War and the Indian Rebellion of 1857.</p><p>Other states took them up. Captain Józef Bem experimented with them in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a>, and Polish rocketeers broke Russian cavalry charges at the Battle of Olszynka Grochowska on 25 February 1831. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> fired them in several battles of the war of 1865–1870.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Rockets were quick to set up and fire, and their shrill whistle and smoke trails could make horses bolt, but wind and instability scattered them by hundreds of yards and duds were frequent. They remained in regular use until the 1850s, when William Hale's spinning design replaced them, and from the 1870s they carried rescue lines to ships in distress.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Congreve rocket” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The first serious check came at the Battle of Katamansu in 1826, where a British-led coalition of coastal peoples, aided by <b>Congreve rockets</b>, defeated an Asante army that had advanced to the coast after the Nsamankow victory.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Mysore's iron-cased rockets impressed British officers and influenced the later <b>Congreve rockets</b> used by the British army.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mysore-1399-1799">Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799) · Military</a></li></ul></section>
